+EchoForge is a strict-open synthetic benchmark for low-altitude uncrewed aerial system (UAS) radar and multimodal sensing research. It asks one primary question: under a rare-positive, low-false-alarm operating constraint, does a locked Engineered Intelligence (EI) sparse-fusion candidate improve the main KPI over an \fusionbaseline? The benchmark run contains 10,000 scenario groups, 50 positive groups, and 30,000 phase records; the 4,500-record blind holdout contains 24 positive records from eight positive scenario groups and is scored once after train/CV selection lock. The primary KPI is \mainkpi. EI improves \mainkpi{} from \kpigain{0.083} to \kpigain{0.699}: a \kpigain{+61.6 percentage-point} gain, \kpigain{+742\%} relative lift, or \kpigain{8.42$\times$} the baseline value. The point \pointkpi{} rises from \kpigain{0.292} to \kpigain{0.833} (\kpigain{+54.1 pp}, \kpigain{+185\%}, \kpigain{2.85$\times$}), average precision rises from \kpigain{0.128} to \kpigain{0.825} (about \kpigain{+545\%}), and selected-threshold false positives fall from \kpigain{49 to 1} (\kpigain{98.0\% fewer false alarms}). \kpicaveat{EI ROC AUC is 0.917, below prior fusion's 0.938}, so the claim is improved low-FPR operating behavior on this declared synthetic public-proxy benchmark, not universal rank dominance. The positive class is a \proxypositive{} with explicit uncertainty; these results are reproducible benchmark evidence, not measured truth, named-platform equivalence, sensor parity, or operational performance.
